  "text": "Allergies:\nPatient recorded as having No Known Allergies to Drugs\n\nChief Complaint:\nSeizure\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\nThe patient is a ___ year old previously right handed man with a \nhistory of bilateral strokes with subsequent right face/arm \nhemiplegia and Broca's aphasia, subsequent seizure disorder, \nhypertension, and hyperlipidemia who presented to an OSH after a \nfall at home which was reportedly due to a seizure,\nand then had 30 seconds of a GTC event at the OSH.\n\nThe following history is obtained from the patient's son, ___ \n___ via ___. Today the patient fell down sometime \nbefore 1:00 pm possibly due to a seizure. This was unwitnessed, \nbut the patient's other son heard a thud and found him on the \nfloor reportedly having a seizure (but he is unsure the details\nof the seizure but he presumes it was a blank stare, and he is \nunsure how long it lasted). EMS was called but the patient did \nnot want to go to the hospital. The patient reportedly had a \nsecond seizure, and EMS was called and took him to the hospital \nthat time.\n\nHe has not had a prior seizure since ___ or ___.  Prior seizures were characterized by the patient's head \nslumping over, slight head shaking, and staring lasting ___ \nminutes. He was kicking with one of his legs (? left leg) at his \nson after the event, but there was no post-ictal slowing or \nsleepiness. He did not remember the event. His son does not \nthink he had loss of\nbowel/bladder function or tongue biting. He does not get \nrhythmic shaking of his arms or legs. His seizures do not \nusually cluster, and he usually has one at a time. He reports \nthat they are \"not epileptic seizures but are mental seizures.\" \nHis seizures are often brought on by anxiety and frustration \nthat he cannot convey what he wants to get across to people. He \nhas not missed any\nrecent Dilantin or Zonegran doses. He often has tremors and \nshaking of his arms. His son says that his seizures are \"annual\" \nand only occur between ___ to after the holidays. His son \ndoes not know when the seizures started, but he thinks they \nstarted after the stroke. His son thinks his neurologist, Dr. \n___, is at ___. His son is not sure if \nhe has ever had an EEG. He reportedly sued his original PCP \nbecause he was not placed on Coumadin in the setting of having \nmultiple TIAs and ended up having a large stroke.\n\nThe patient himself says that he is here for back pain and not \nfor a seizure. He denies any foul tastes/smells, loss of \nbowel/bladder function, or shaking of his arms/legs. \n\nAt the OSH, vitals on admission were temp 98.5, bp 135/88, HR \n75, RR 20, SaO2 96% on RA. The patient's chief complaint was \nthat he had \"fallen 2 times this AM\". He was s/p fall at home 4 \nweeks ago with worsening left flank pain since then. Per the \nnotes, he was thought to have a mechanical injury causing back \nstrain. Exam showed that he could only answer yes/no and had \nbaseline right\nhemiplegia. He was ordered a head CT, and while being placed in \nthe room had an approximately 30 second tonic clonic seizure for \nwhich he was given Ativan 1 mg IV. Neurology was consulted via \nphone and recommend giving Keppra 1000 mg IV x1. Head CT \nreportedly showed no acute intracranial process, old right\nparietotemporal infarct and dilated ventricles somewhat out of \nproportion to sulci. CT abdomen/pelvis showed no acute abdominal \nor pelvic process, disc degeneration with bulging disc material \nat levels L3-S1. He was given Tylenol 1 gram PO x1 and Morphine \n4 mg IV, and transferred to ___ for further neurological \nevaluation.\n\nOn ROS, he has not had fevers/chills, but he has been having \nsome coughing and sneezing. The patient did have an unwitnessed \nfall 2 weeks ago with subsquent back pain, and his son does not \nthink that was due to a seizure. His son reports that he was \nstumbling when walking yesterday and appeared off balance.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nThe patient is a ___ year old previously right handed man with a \nhistory of bilateral strokes with subsequent right face/arm \nhemiplegia and Broca's aphasia, subsequent seizure disorder, \nhypertension, and hyperlipidemia who presented to an OSH after a \nfall at home which was reportedly due to a seizure, and then had \n30 seconds of a GTC event at the OSH. His son reports that his \ntypical seizures are head slumping over, slight head shaking, \nand staring, and after the event he starts kicking his son \nwithout post-ictal sleepiness. There is no rhythmic shaking of \nhis arms/legs, foul taste/smell, tongue biting, or loss of \nbowel/bladder. His son says the seizures \"mental\", are usually \nnear the holidays, and are triggered by anxiety and\nfrustration. He does not admit to missing any medication doses \nin the past month. His exam is significant for Broca's aphasia \nbut the patient can sing, right face and arm hemiplegia, \npostural tremor, and right arm hyperreflexia. The patient may \nhave a\nseizure disorder especially since 2 of his strokes appear \ncortically based on the head CT. His Dilantin level is \nsubtherapeutic at 8.4 despite being on large doses of Dilantin, \nso this could have been a trigger for the event. However, given \nthe son's descriptions of the events, he may also have \nnon-epileptic seizures. \n\n- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - \n- - - - - - - - - - ___ Course:\n\nMr. ___ was admitted following his initial presentation to \nthe ER and load with Dilantin.  His serum level of dilantin took \na surprisingly long time to increase following his presentation, \nwith further increases in his daily dose required.  His Coumadin \nand Dilantin may be interacting to decrease his serum Dilantin.  \nAt the time of admission he was supratherapeutic on Coumdadin \n(INR 3.1), so this medication was held throughout the admission \nand at the time of discharge.  He was instructed to follow-up \nwith his ___ clinic two days following discharge (on a \n___, and to convey the results of his testing to his PCP so \nthat he could advise Mr. ___ on restarting ___.  I \nspoke with Mr. ___ PCP and conveyed this plan to him \ndirectly, and he agreed to follow-up on this INR.\n  No seizures were recorded during the ~___ he was on EEG.  It \nremains unclear whether Mr. ___ seizures are epileptic or \nnon-electrographic, though numerous features of his story \nsuggest they are not epileptic events (see above).  With his \nsub-therapeutic Dilantin level, however, he certainly was not \nprotected from any true epileptic events.  He was discharge on \nan increased dose of Dilantin of 250 mg in the morning and 300 \nmg in the evening.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nEpilepsy, breakthrough seizures",
